87. If the data of the briquette are, as in § 86, the volumes of the minor briquettes, but the condition as to close contact is not satisfied, we have

{{block center|<math>\int_{x_0}^{x_m}\int_{y_0}^{y_n} x^py^qu\,dx\,dy = \text{K} + \text{L} + \text{R} - x^p_my^q_n\sigma_{0,0}</math>}}

where <math>\text{K} \equiv x^p_m \times q</math>th moment with regard to plane <math>y=0</math>, <math>\text{L} \equiv y^q_n \times p</math>th moment with regard to plane <math>x=0</math>, and <math>\text{R}</math> is the volume of a briquette whose ordinate at <math>(x_r, y_s)</math> is found by multiplying by <math>pq\ x_r^{p-1}\ y_s^{q-1}</math> the volume of that portion of the original briquette which lies between the planes <math>x=x_0</math>, <math>x=x_r</math>, <math>y=y_0</math>, <math>y=y_s</math>. The ordinates of this new briquette at the points of intersection of <math>x=x_0</math>, <math>x=x_1</math>, . . . with <math>y=y_0</math>, <math>y=y_1</math>, . . . are obtained from the data by summation and multiplication; and the ordinary methods then apply for calculation of its volume. Either or both of the expressions <math>\text{K}</math> and <math>\text{L}</math> will have to be calculated by means of the formula of § 84; if this is applied to both expressions, we have a formula which may be written in a more general form

{{block center|<math>\int_a^b\int_p^q u\phi(x, y)dx\,dy = \int_a^b\int_p^q u\,dx\,dy\ .\ \phi(b, q)</math>}}

{{block center|<math>-\int_a^b \left\{ \int_a^x\int_p^q u\,dx\,dy \right\} \frac{d\phi(x, q)}{dx}\,dx</math>}}

{{block center|<math>-\int_p^q \left\{ \int_a^b\int_p^y u\,dx\,dy \right\} \frac{d\phi(b, y)}{dy}\,dy</math>}}

{{block center|<math>+\int_a^b\int_p^q \left\{ \int_a^x\int_b^y u\,dx\,dy \right\} \frac{d^2\phi(x, y)}{dx\,dy}\,dx\,dy.</math>}}

